---
import BaseLayout from '~/components/BaseLayout.astro';
import Matrix from '~/components/bench/Matrix.astro';
import Provenance from '~/components/bench/Provenance.astro';
import ResultsTable from '~/components/bench/ResultsTable.astro';
import { SITE } from '~/data/site';
import { CAMPAIGNS, frontierFor, type Campaign } from '~/data/benchmarks';

const campaign = (id: string): Campaign => {
  const found = CAMPAIGNS.find((c) => c.id === id);
  if (!found) throw new Error(`unknown campaign ${id}`);
  return found;
};

const jsonC = campaign('json-c-2026-09-25');
const deficits = frontierFor({ only: ['json'] });
---

<BaseLayout
  title="JSON benchmarks — yeptris"
  description="JSON documents through yeptris: the strict fused RFC 8259 parse path measured against libyaml and rapidyaml on JSON-shaped corpora, with the JSONTestSuite conformance gate."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label"><a href="/benchmarks/">Benchmarks</a> · JSON</p>
      <h1 class="sec-title">JSON, through <em>yeptris</em></h1>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        yeptris parses JSON as what it is — a strict subset of YAML 1.2 —
        through a dedicated fused walk that validates the RFC 8259 grammar
        and builds the document in a single pass. Conformance is gated by
        the JSONTestSuite corpus under the YAML 1.2 schema, where yeptris
        accepts all 95 valid inputs and rejects all 188 invalid inputs.
        The measurements below race the JSON-shaped corpora through the
        committed benchmark matrix against the YAML incumbents and against
        simdjson, the reference JSON parser, in the same run. Two
        consumption routes are measured against simdjson: DOM
        construction, which builds a navigable document, and the tape
        route introduced in yeptris 0.2.2, which validates into compact
        parallel columns with no document model. The simdjson comparison
        is stated with both numbers and is the honest centerpiece of this
        page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:1.6rem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Matrix tech="json" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-tint-periwinkle lang-sec" id="c">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">C</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">yeptris versus <em>the YAML incumbents</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        The matrix generates four JSON-shaped corpora: a multi-document
        flow corpus, a single-document flow corpus, one large flat
        document, and a record-heavy users corpus. Each is measured
        through DOM construction, pull parsing and event recording, in
        addition to emission, in the same run against the same-build
        reference libraries.
      </p>

      <ResultsTable
        rows={jsonC.results
          .filter((r) => r.competitor === 'libyaml')
          .map((result) => ({ result, campaign: jsonC }))}
        competitorHead="libyaml 0.2.5"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={jsonC} />

      <h3>Versus rapidyaml on the same corpora</h3>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={jsonC.results
          .filter((r) => r.competitor === 'ryml')
          .map((result) => ({ result, campaign: jsonC }))}
        competitorHead="ryml 0.16.0"
      />
      <p>
        The head-to-head against rapidyaml is run interleaved with a
        fair-order referee and reports the median of rounds. The yeptris
        performance ledger records the engine-dispatch work behind these
        rows, together with every measured dead end, in the
        <a href={`${SITE.githubYaml}/blob/main/benchmarks/PERF-LEDGER.md`}>yeptris ledger</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Versus simdjson, the JSON-native reference</h3>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={jsonC.results
          .filter((r) => r.competitor === 'simdjson')
          .map((result) => ({ result, campaign: jsonC }))}
        competitorHead="simdjson 3.13.0"
      />
      <p>
        simdjson validates JSON into a linear tape with SIMD structural
        indexing and no document model — a fundamentally cheaper contract
        than anything that also serves YAML. The referee is calibrated to
        simdjson&rsquo;s own harness idiom: it parses a pre-built padded
        string, so no input-copy tax sits in its timed loop (the
        calibration itself is ledger history — an earlier version of this
        table flattered us). Calibrated, the DOM route measures 0.70×
        simdjson on the flat document shape and 0.95× on the record-heavy
        shape — the bracket of per-member overhead the ledger&rsquo;s
        slices have been cutting — while the packed-record tape route
        trades tree materialization for bulk access. The walk is the
        residual, recorded slice by slice in the ledger.
      </p>

      <p class="docs-canonical">
        To reproduce: configure yeptris with
        <code>-DYEPTRIS_BUILD_BENCHMARKS=ON -DYEPTRIS_BENCH_LIBYAML_ROOT=…</code>
        (and <code>-DYEPTRIS_BENCH_RYML_ROOT=…</code> for the
        head-to-head), then run <code>bench_matrix --full</code>. The
        harness is committed at
        <a href={`${SITE.githubYaml}/tree/main/benchmarks`}>benchmarks</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="deficits">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">JSON</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Measured <em>deficits</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        Every JSON operation in the result data that remains behind its
        competitor, ordered from the weakest ratio. This section is
        generated from the same result files as the tables above.
      </p>
      {deficits.length > 0 && <ResultsTable rows={deficits} competitorHead="versus" />}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lang-sec {
    padding-top: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 3rem;
  }
</style>
